    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does water hardness affect water-miscible cutting fluids?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Water hardness is caused by dissolved calcium (Ca2+) and magnesium (Mg2+) ions. In an emulsion, these divalent cations chemically bond with anionic emulsifying surfactants to form insoluble soap scum. This depletes the active surfactants holding the oil in suspension, causing the oil droplets to coalesce and separate (emulsion splitting), leading to rust on machine tools and rapid microbiological spoilage."
      }
